  "analysis": "Market read: yesterday was risk-off — broad and defensive assets fell (A06 -0.6%, A08 -0.6%, A09 -0.8%, A03 -0.9%, A13 -1.2%) while beaten-down tech stocks bounced (A01 +2.3%, A14 +2.1%). Over 20d the pattern is clear: tech/emerging/gold sold off (A11 -9.8%, A12 -10.5%, A05 -6.0%, A01 -13.6%) while health and financials led (A04 +10.1%, A08 +5.9%, A07 +21.8%). The portfolio is only -1.72% from peak, so no drawdown actions are triggered. Forecast medians carry no directional skill, so I lean on interval widths and portfolio construction rather than chasing recent moves. The baseline already concentrates in the narrow-interval assets (5d widths: A06 3.9%, A09 3.9%, A02 4.4%, A08 4.6%), and the current book's overweights sit in exactly those names — I endorse keeping them. The widest-interval, highest-vol names (A01 12.2%, A14 11.4%, A07 10.5%, A13 9.2%, A12 7.9%) stay at zero/low; their bounces are not signals. Main change: raise gold from 1.88% to 4.0% (still below its 4.74% baseline) funded from cash (12.19% -> 10.07%, still above the 10% floor), because with ~74% in equities gold is the only diversifier besides bonds and cash, and the mild drawdown plus yesterday's risk-off tape argues for a bit more ballast. Concentration checks: us_broad 20.7%, financials 18.3%, every group under 30%; largest ETF 12.9% under the 25% cap; largest stock 6.1% under 10%; cash 10.07% >= 10%. No other changes — sub-2pp moves would not trade anyway and I have no concrete edge elsewhere.",
